  "synthesis_conclusions": [
    "Brief contact with natural environments is associated with higher levels of positive affect (moderate magnitude) and lower levels of negative affect (smaller magnitude, consistent across studies) relative to comparison conditions.",
    "The beneficial effects of nature on emotional well-being are primarily driven by increases in positive affect and, to a lesser extent, decreases in negative affect.",
    "Contact with nature provides substantial benefits for emotional well-being, even in small doses, suggesting it can be an additional route to increase subjective well-being."
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "Further research is needed to assess whether findings generalize to studies using alternative research designs.",
    "Future research should use larger samples to provide more accurate effect size estimates.",
    "More research is needed on the duration of positive effects of nature exposure over extended periods.",
    "Generalizability of findings to more traditional cultures and developing nations needs to be determined.",
    "Additional research should address nature's impact on other facets of well-being beyond emotional components.",
    "Future research should examine discrete emotional responses to natural environments to provide a more nuanced picture of well-being effects.",
    "Future research should examine affective responses to natural environments that systematically vary in the presence of evolutionarily significant resources and hazards."
  ],
  "risk_of_bias_assessment": "Duval and Tweedie's trim and fill approach and Egger's weighted regression test were used. For positive affect, there was a slight publication bias by underrepresenting small effect sizes. For negative affect, there was a lack of bias.",
